==============================================================================
(3) B2 (square, order-4, s=0) vs A2/E2 (hexagonal, order-6, s!=0): the margin's source
==============================================================================
  SQUARE/B2: order-4 h (h^2=-1 mod k) -> s(h,k)=0 -> would give ZERO margin (degenerate):
    k= 5: h=2 (h^2=-1): s=0  [B2, margin 0]
    k=13: h=5 (h^2=-1): s=0  [B2, margin 0]
    k=17: h=4 (h^2=-1): s=0  [B2, margin 0]
    k=25: h=7 (h^2=-1): s=0  [B2, margin 0]
    k=29: h=12 (h^2=-1): s=0  [B2, margin 0]
  HEXAGONAL/A2: order-6 n (n^3=-1 mod Phi6) -> s=-(Phi6-1)/12Phi6 != 0 -> POSITIVE margin:
    Phi6(4)= 13: s(n,Phi6)=-1/13  [A2, margin=3/52]
    Phi6(7)= 43: s(n,Phi6)=-7/86  [A2, margin=6/301]
    Phi6(8)= 57: s(n,Phi6)=-14/171  [A2, margin=7/456]
    Phi6(14)=183: s(n,Phi6)=-91/1098  [A2, margin=13/2562]

==============================================================================
(4) E2 weight-2 Eisenstein forms on Gamma_0(14): F_d = E2(tau)-d E2(d tau), d|14
==============================================================================
  F_ 2 = E2-2E2(2t): const=  -1  q-exp[0..8]=[-1, -24, -24, -96, -24, -144, -96, -192, -24]
  F_ 7 = E2-7E2(7t): const=  -6  q-exp[0..8]=[-6, -24, -72, -96, -168, -144, -288, -24, -360]
  F_14 = E2-14E2(14t): const= -13  q-exp[0..8]=[-13, -24, -72, -96, -168, -144, -288, -192, -360]
  const(F_14) = 1-14 = -(n-1) = -13.  F_2,F_7,F_14 span the 3-dim Eisenstein subspace
  (X_0(14) has 4 cusps => dim = c-1 = 3); the 1-dim cusp form f_14 (genus 1) = the residual.
  The apex cusp d=7 (Atkin-Lehner W_7, width 2) is where f_14 must be controlled = the hard core.

